msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/productivity/ai/access_policy.rst:29
msgid "AI access rule"
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/productivity/ai/access_policy.rst:31
msgid "An ``ai.access.rule`` targets:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/productivity/ai/access_policy.rst:33
msgid "optional :guilabel:`Access group` (empty = **global** rule for all AI users);"
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/productivity/ai/access_policy.rst:34
msgid ":guilabel:`Model` (required);"
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/productivity/ai/access_policy.rst:35
msgid "optional :guilabel:`Field` (empty = model-level rule);"
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/productivity/ai/access_policy.rst:36
msgid ":guilabel:`perm_read` / :guilabel:`perm_write` / :guilabel:`perm_create` / :guilabel:`perm_delete` each as **Inherit**, **Allow** or **Deny**."
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/productivity/ai/access_policy.rst:39
msgid "Create and delete are model-level only (ignored on field rules). Setting field values on create still needs write allow on those fields where the policy checks them."
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/productivity/ai/access_policy.rst:44
msgid "Resolution order"
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/productivity/ai/access_policy.rst:46
msgid "For a given user, model, operation and optional field, ``ai_can`` resolves roughly as:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/productivity/ai/access_policy.rst:49
msgid "**Hard-deny floor** — technical / self-protection models always deny, including every ``ai.*`` platform model and raw ``mail.message`` / ``discuss.channel`` (see :doc:`security`)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/productivity/ai/access_policy.rst:52
msgid "**Any explicit Deny**, field-level or model-level. A Deny at either tier is absolute and nothing below overrides it."
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/productivity/ai/access_policy.rst:54
msgid "**Field-level Allow** (read/write only) — decides the field tier and outranks the global default for that field. This is the mechanism the shipped rules and the whole cross-model opt-in rely on: with write defaulting to Deny, a field-scoped Allow is what makes a field writable. It does not replace the model tier, though: a **direct** write to a model must still pass step 4 or 6 for that model. Only a payload nested inside a parent one2many or many2many field skips the model check on the child model."
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/productivity/ai/access_policy.rst:61
msgid "**Model-level Allow** — most specific Allow wins among groups."
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/productivity/ai/access_policy.rst:62
msgid "**Explicit-allow-only models** — ``ir.attachment`` and ``mail.activity`` stop here. They are off the floor, so a rule can open them, but they never fall through to the global defaults: with no matching Allow they are denied."
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/productivity/ai/access_policy.rst:65
msgid "**Global defaults** from Settings (default_read / create / write / delete)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/productivity/ai/access_policy.rst:67
msgid "Unknown models or operations fail closed. Results are cached and invalidated when rules, groups or parameters change."
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/productivity/ai/access_policy.rst:71
msgid "Universal sanitiser rails (before/alongside policy)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/productivity/ai/access_policy.rst:73
msgid "Independently of allow rules, the gate refuses or strips the following — with one exception, noted in the list:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/productivity/ai/access_policy.rst:76
msgid "magic fields;"
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/productivity/ai/access_policy.rst:77
msgid "sensitive-looking field names (password, token, api_key, …);"
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/productivity/ai/access_policy.rst:78
msgid "fields that write into another model — the only rail an Allow rule can lift, and only per field; see :ref:`ai/policy/cross-model`;"
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/productivity/ai/access_policy.rst:80
msgid "lifecycle ``state`` writes;"
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/productivity/ai/access_policy.rst:81
msgid "non-writable / complex types that must not be LLM-driven;"
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/productivity/ai/access_policy.rst:82
msgid "hard-deny models in relational paths / domains."
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/productivity/ai/access_policy.rst:87
msgid "Cross-model write protection"
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/productivity/ai/access_policy.rst:89
msgid "Some Odoo fields do not only store a value: writing them also writes into a **different** model. ``crm.lead.email_from``, for example, updates the linked partner's email address. Left unguarded, an assistant allowed to \"edit leads\" could change contact data outside the models you opened, and the audit log would only name the lead."
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/productivity/ai/access_policy.rst:95
msgid "Odoo has two kinds of such field, and only one of them can be opened."
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/productivity/ai/access_policy.rst:97
msgid "A **related** field re-routes the write to the other model by definition. The gate refuses it outright, on create and on write, and no access rule of any shape opens it — allow the assistant to write the *target* model instead."
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/productivity/ai/access_policy.rst:101
msgid "A field carrying an **inverse** runs arbitrary code that may touch anything: ``crm.lead.email_from`` is compute-plus-inverse, and its inverse writes ``res.partner.email``. The gate refuses such a field **unless a field-level rule explicitly allows writing that exact field**. A model-level Allow is deliberately *not* enough: granting write on ``crm.lead`` must not silently open ``res.partner`` as well."
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/productivity/ai/access_policy.rst:108
msgid "The surface is wider than it looks. Core Odoo carries inverses on everyday fields — a vendor bill's partner, journal, currency and payment term, an invoice line's product and account, a lead's email — so expect to meet this rail on the first real write you enable."
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/productivity/ai/access_policy.rst:113
msgid "Either refusal is a configuration boundary, not abuse: it records no violation and costs the user no strike."
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/productivity/ai/access_policy.rst:117
msgid "Opening one of these fields"
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/productivity/ai/access_policy.rst:119
msgid "Go to :menuselection:`AI --> Configuration --> Security --> Access Rules`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/productivity/ai/access_policy.rst:120
msgid "Create a rule with :guilabel:`Model` **and** :guilabel:`Field` set — the field is what makes it count."
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/productivity/ai/access_policy.rst:122
msgid "Set :guilabel:`perm_write` = **Allow**."
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/productivity/ai/access_policy.rst:125
msgid "A rule with :guilabel:`Field` left empty is a model-level rule and will not lift this rail, however permissive it looks. :guilabel:`perm_write` is the permission that counts, and it is also what opens the field inside a *create* payload: :guilabel:`perm_create` is model-level and is ignored on field rules."
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/productivity/ai/access_policy.rst:130
msgid "The rule lifts the inverse rail and nothing else. A field that is not stored in the database — ``product.template.standard_price`` is computed with an inverse but has no column — and a related field both stay refused however permissive the rule is. So if the refusal survives a correct field-level rule, the field is related or not stored, not an inverse field."
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/productivity/ai/access_policy.rst:137
msgid "When the refusal happens inside a one2many or many2many payload — an invoice line inside :guilabel:`Invoice lines`, for example — the assistant reports only the **parent** field, ``invoice_line_ids``, because that is the field it tried to write. The nested model and field that were actually refused go to the Odoo **server** log at INFO level; search it for ``AI x2many write refused``. They do not appear under :menuselection:`AI --> Monitoring --> Logs`, and a refusal for a missing field opt-in records no violation either — it is a configuration boundary, not abuse."
msgstr ""

